and as the tory leadership race  continues candidates uh coming under  renewed pressure to address the cost of  living crisis so let's get more from the  former chief whip and the supporter of  rishi sunak mark harper who joins us now  live mark harper good to have you with  us uh good morning to you so  are we going to see uh rasheed sunik and  liz trust sitting down with the current  prime minister to try and put a plan in  place before the new person that takes  over number 10  no i don't think that's going to happen  but i think what is very important is  that rishi's made it clear  that we're looking at energy bills  likely to be higher  than what were we were expecting when he  set out his plans when he was chancellor  in may and what he said is that that  therefore will require more help being  delivered um and he's going to set that  out in detail uh once we know exactly  what the new energy price cap will be in  october but he's got he's going to see  he's set out but it's going to be along  the lines of the framework that he  already has used so a mix of  rebates on bills and direct support to  those people who are the poorest and  most vulnerable by contrast  liz trustees has said that she's not  going to deliver what she calls handouts  and she's only going to cut tax and the  problem with that is it gives a a big  tax cut to the prime minister  1810 pounds a year but only 59 pounds to  somebody working full time on the  national living wage and nothing at all  to pensioners so i think what rich is  setting out is the right plan which i  hope will provide a loss of reassurance  and comfort to those who are worrying  this winter about how they're going to  pay their energy bills yeah of course so  we'll come onto this dressing room but  we want to push you on this why don't  you think they'll sit down i mean i know  that gordon brown suggested it i know  that it's been suggested again by davia  earlier today you're saying that they're  not going to do it but many people  watching will be wondering why during a  time when we're expecting bills to go up  so massively and people are struggling  to understand how they're going to pay  for those bills you guys aren't sitting  down to try and work out a plan  that doesn't involve starting after the  5th of september  well  very clearly there's a fundamental  difference of opinion between the two  leadership candidates so rishi thinks  that you we are going to need to give  direct support to people um because if  you're going to help the poorest and  most vulnerable you can't just cut their  taxes because they don't pay a lot of  tax uh let's trust things that you can  simply cut uh national insurance but  that gives a big boost to people on the  biggest incomes doesn't help pensioners  at all and gives very little help to  people on the national living wage who  are working full-time so there's a big  difference of opinion there what will  happen  is that the civil service will work out  both  options so that they'll be ready for  whoever's going to win and become prime  minister on the 6th of september but  rishi thought it was very important that  we give people some peace of mind so  he's made it clear that if he were  elected  he would use the frameworks that he's  already set out and would deliver more  help because it's clear that energy  prices are going to be i think about 600  pounds higher we're expecting than we  were expecting back in may and he'll set  out the details of that one's off gem  have confirmed the level of the price  cap which will be at the end of this  month yeah i mean you call it a  difference of opinion but people  watching will be thinking and feeling  anxious that they're going to have to  just wait until they see how much this  price cap is going to increase by how  much more their bills are going to  increase by while you guys are concerned  about opinions and it feels to me like  it's ego that means you can't get in the  room together  well not at all it's just a very  different of approach rishi i think is  setting out the right approach and i  think people can judge him on his track  record so when he became chancellor just  a month later we were faced with the  kogit pandemic and millions of people  were scared about their jobs and about  their businesses um he got the treasury  to respond within days and rolled out a  very significant plan people remember  the furlough  and all the money to help keep  businesses uh going  and they they still remember that and i  think they can look at his track record  he had their backs during that crisis  and when he was chancellor early this  year  he rolled out the package of support  that by the way currently hasn't yet  been delivered people haven't had the  discounts off their bills yet that kicks  in  in october we have delivered the first  level of support to the poorest and most  vulnerable there's more of that still to  come so there is actually a lot of help  in the system that rishi put in place  when he was chancellor of the exchequer  yeah but it is acceptance by you must  happen that it will be inadequate with  the rise that we're expecting to come  and i do wonder further on uh liz trust  and her um  candidacy you haven't held back this  morning in in criticizing her plans and  you haven't held back on social media  criticizing her plans so i do wonder how  do you expect to be able to work  potentially in a government that is run  by liz trust  well two things first of all in a  leadership election the different  candidates set out their stalls and and  challenge each other on the positions  they set out and then when that  leadership election is over  uh whoever wins obviously everyone comes  together uh i happen to think that  russia has a very good chance i feel  sorry mr harper just doing suggestions i  feel a little bit more than that doesn't  it right now because obviously this is  the new prime minister and you are  speaking to the country and this person  will be leading our country through what  may very well be a very difficult winter  for people economically  yes and in fact it's because it's such a  going to be such a difficult winter and  because the single most important thing  that the government is going to have to  deal with this is is this economic  crisis that i'm supporting the candidate  who i think has got the best plan to  help people through the winter we are  going to have to give people direct  support liz trust has ruled out giving  out what she calls handouts that means  leaving pensioners and the poorest and  most vulnerable without the help that  they're going to need to get through  this winter and to pay their energy  bills rishi tsunak thinks that we do  need to help them um and i think that  that message is going to land with  conservative members conservative  members want a government that supports  the most vulnerable and those who need  our help to get them through the winter  and i think in the end that's why  they'll end up supporting rishi soon  after why he'll win this contest and  become prime minister on the 6th of  september  now as you've seen this morning in the  times the justice secretary and deputy  prime minister dominic raab has said  that if  conservative party members follow liz  trust and vote for her they'll be  writing an electoral suicide note do you  agree that  well i do i think if if a government was  elect if she were elected and she  weren't to help the poorest and most  vulnerable people in society which is  what she said she said she's not going  to give them direct support then i think  that would be enormously damaging to our  prospects the conservative party has to  be rooted in reality it has to look at  the situation  facing the country it's a massive  economic challenge and i think you need  somebody who's got the grip  and the experience to deal with that  economic challenge  the voters think that rishi has that  economic experience that's why swing  voters those voters who voted for us in  2019 who aren't currently planning on  voting for us uh massively support him  over liz drussie has a 20-point lead  amongst those voters and if we want to  win that election when it comes then i  think rishi sunak is the man to put into  downing street  and you're happy with the kind of  language use  suicide notes  well i think if the government doesn't  help the poorest and most vulnerable  people to get through the winter to pay  their energy bills and to put food on  the table then i think that will be  electrically very damaging which is why  rishi said more help is needed and you  referred to giving people peace of mind  he's set out the fact that if he becomes  prime minister there will be more help  uh he people can rely on that assurance  looking at his track record through the  pandemic and through the energy prices  he's already delivered when he was  chancellor and i think that will provide  reassurance and peace of mind to people  as they're contemplating what is going  to be a very difficult winter for many  many people

